WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.530
Here's someone a lot of us know.

2
00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.966
A case manager, two years with a full

3
00:00:03.966 --> 00:00:05.500
caseload, and really good at it.

4
00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.582
Lately they answer email at ten at night,

5
00:00:07.582 --> 00:00:09.495
skip lunch and feel flat even after a

6
00:00:09.495 --> 00:00:10.690
good outcome for a family.

7
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.180
Nothing's on fire.

8
00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.170
The work is getting done.

9
00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:15.740
So is there actually a problem?

10
00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:17.070
That's the trap.

11
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.880
The early signs are quiet.

12
00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.188
Feeling flat after a win is one of

13
00:00:21.188 --> 00:00:21.430
them.

14
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.385
The World Health Organization describes burnout as a

15
00:00:24.385 --> 00:00:27.049
result of workplace stress that hasn't been managed:

16
00:00:27.049 --> 00:00:29.714
exhaustion, growing distance from the work and feeling

17
00:00:29.714 --> 00:00:30.380
less effective.

18
00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.581
So it's about the conditions at work, not

19
00:00:33.581 --> 00:00:34.470
a personal failing.

20
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:35.440
Right.

21
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.036
And that matters, because it means some of

22
00:00:38.036 --> 00:00:40.342
the fixes are things a team can change

23
00:00:40.342 --> 00:00:40.630
together.

24
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.660
Okay, practical moment.

25
00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:44.630
It's 9:45 at night.

26
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.224
A colleague emails a non-urgent question about next

27
00:00:48.224 --> 00:00:49.020
week's schedule.

28
00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.630
I'm going to answer it.

29
00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.160
It takes thirty seconds.

30
00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:53.250
It does.

31
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.593
And it also tells your colleague that late-night

32
00:00:55.593 --> 00:00:56.620
answers are normal here.

33
00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.370
Next week, they'll feel they should answer at

34
00:00:59.370 --> 00:00:59.990
ten, too.

35
00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:00.740
Huh.

36
00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.990
So the thirty seconds isn't really the cost.

37
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:04.080
No.

38
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.659
One small protection is to schedule the reply

39
00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:08.909
for the morning, or simply answer it at

40
00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:09.190
nine.

41
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.980
The question still gets answered.

42
00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:12.330
The norm doesn't spread.

43
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.540
What about when it's more than email?

44
00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:17.497
Caseload up by a third since spring, two

45
00:01:17.497 --> 00:01:20.003
reports late, and I haven't said anything to

46
00:01:20.003 --> 00:01:20.630
my supervisor.

47
00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.237
That's the moment to raise it, while it's

48
00:01:23.237 --> 00:01:24.360
two reports and not ten.

49
00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.128
Bring facts and a question: here's what's changed,

50
00:01:27.128 --> 00:01:29.626
here's what's slipping, which of these should I

51
00:01:29.626 --> 00:01:30.250
put first?

52
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.613
That's much easier to say than, I'm not

53
00:01:33.613 --> 00:01:33.940
coping.

54
00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.230
And it's more useful to a supervisor.

55
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.460
They can actually decide something.

56
00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:39.990
Last one.

57
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.670
A colleague has seemed withdrawn for a few

58
00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.060
weeks and left early twice without saying goodbye.

59
00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.470
I don't want to pry.

60
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.520
You don't have to diagnose anything.

61
00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.461
A private, simple check-in works: I've noticed you

62
00:01:51.461 --> 00:01:53.030
seem a bit quieter lately.

63
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.200
How are you doing?

64
00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:56.850
Then listen, and let them decide how much

65
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:57.390
to share.

66
00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.920
And if they're really struggling?

67
00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:01.550
Know where support lives.

68
00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.163
Minnesota's state Employee Assistance Program is free, voluntary

69
00:02:05.163 --> 00:02:08.346
and confidential, for state employees and the people

70
00:02:08.346 --> 00:02:09.540
they live with.

71
00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.842
And if someone may be in crisis, the

72
00:02:12.842 --> 00:02:15.875
988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline answers by call,

73
00:02:15.875 --> 00:02:17.770
text or chat, any time.

74
00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.140
So the course isn't about fixing anyone.

75
00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:23.352
It's about noticing early, and knowing the next

76
00:02:23.352 --> 00:02:23.710
step.
